// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT OR Apache-2.0
// Donor: Roslyn's `EatToken` / `EatTokenAsKind` pair for the QUESTION -- a
// parser that reads broken input needs TWO answers to "the token is not here",
// and they are not the same answer. NOT taken: Roslyn's skipped-token trivia
// and its error-recovery ranking; this subset plants one lossless Err leaf and
// lets `check` do the talking.
// Role: layer 2 (parse) -- taking a token that is EXPECTED at this position,
// and the difference between "optional here" and "required here". That
// difference is the whole of registry #809: for a year the closing bracket was
// taken by the optional door, and `fn main() {` with no `}` compiled with code
// 0 and not one diagnostic. Sixteen call sites carried it.
// Used by: parse.nv, expr.nv, decls.nv -- every place a comma, a keyword or a
// bracket is taken.
// Guarded by: check-novac-closer-mandatory.py (a closing token through the
// optional door is red, base zero), and the class test
// "an unclosed bracket is refused, not accepted in silence (#809)".
// novac/src/parse/expect.nv -- is this token optional here, or required.

module novac.parse

import ../lex.{TokenKind}
import ../tree.{Node, NodeKind}

/// Take the token if it is the one wanted, and say ONCE what it means when it
/// is not.
///
/// The phrase `if @peek() == X { kids.push(@take()) }` stood at fifty-four
/// places in this parser: every optional comma and keyword. Each was an
/// incomplete branching (P31) whose missing half means the same thing
/// everywhere -- the token is optional here, there is nothing to take, and
/// that is a valid outcome. Fifty-four empty `else` comments would say it
/// fifty-four times; a named door says it once, and the call site gets
/// shorter into the bargain.
///
/// A CLOSING bracket is not one of those, and saying it was is #809: this
/// door used to take `}` too, so a file ending mid-body was accepted in
/// silence. Closers go through `@push_closer` below, and the guard
/// check-novac-closer-mandatory keeps them there.
fn Cursor mut @push_if(mut kids []Node, k TokenKind) -> () {
    if @peek() == k {
        kids.push(@take())
    } else {
        // Nothing to take: the token is optional at this position. This is
        // THE empty branch P31 asks to be named, and it is named here for
        // every call site at once.
    }
}

/// Take the CLOSING token, and REFUSE when it is not there.
///
/// The twin of `@push_if`, and the difference between them is the whole of
/// #809. A comma is optional at its position; a closer is not -- every call
/// site below has already consumed its opener, so the language requires the
/// match. Taken with the optional door, `fn main() {` with no `}` parsed
/// clean and compiled to rc=0 with no diagnostic at all.
///
/// The Err leaf planted here is the one the rest of the parser plants for a
/// run it cannot read, so `check` refuses it through `report_err_branch` with
/// the syntax message and a position: one rule, one shape, one place to
/// change it.
fn Cursor mut @push_closer(mut kids []Node, k TokenKind) -> () {
    if @peek() == k {
        kids.push(@take())
    } else if @at_eof() && !holds_err(kids) {
        // The file ENDED and this construct was whole until it did: the pair
        // that never arrived is the cause, and a zero-width Err keeps the
        // tree lossless while giving the refusal a position. This is the
        // carrier of #809 -- `fn main() {` used to compile clean.
        kids.push(@node(NodeKind.Err, []Node.of(@missing())))
    } else {
        // Silence here, and both halves of the condition earned it by
        // measurement against the oracle (2026-08-30).
        //
        // NOT at end of file: tokens still stand ahead, so the pair went
        // missing because an earlier failure moved the cursor -- the cause is
        // elsewhere and someone else names it (`fn main junk here () { }`:
        // one diagnostic, on the junk).
        //
        // Already failed INSIDE: the missing pair is the wake of that
        // failure, not a second cause (`fn main() { mut i`, `fn main()
        // { "oops }`: one diagnostic each, and the oracle agrees). Two
        // causes at DIFFERENT levels still get two, because only this
        // construct's own subtree is asked.
    }
}

/// Did this construct already fail, at ANY depth? Asked by the closer door and
/// by nobody else: the answer decides whether a missing pair is a cause of its
/// own or the wake of one already named.
///
/// IN DEPTH, and that is a measurement rather than caution: `fn main() { mut i`
/// leaves the block's failure inside the BINDING, not among the block's own
/// children, so a one-level look answered "intact" and the file got two
/// diagnostics where the oracle gives one.
fn holds_err(kids []Node) -> bool {
    for c in kids {
        match c {
            Leaf(_) => continue
            Branch { kind, children, .. } => {
                if kind == NodeKind.Err { return true }
                if holds_err(children) { return true }
            }
        }
    }
    false
}

